 [step:doc-refs]\r\n  # Prose reference rot (WI-062 tiering, wired in by WI-308). check_docs.py proves\r\n  # a markdown LINK resolves; this closes what links cannot see — a backticked\r\n  # repo path or a `sym:module.name` reference that names something no longer\r\n  # there. Wired only NOW, and that order was the point: the check reported 561\r\n  # findings before WI-062 split UNTRACED (explained by a mechanical reason —\r\n> # this repo's gate runs at today (as [step:smoke] does), rather than the\r\n  # template's DevStg-Impl: code changes here at every rung, and a report that\r\n  # never exits nonzero costs a gate run nothing but its lines.\r\n  command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_readability.py --root .\r\n  from-stage = DevStg-Tests\r\n  paths = project-trajectory/scripts/* tests/* docs/stack.ini docs/complexity-baseline docs/flag-axis-baseline\r\n  layer = product\r\n  \r\n  [step:module-coverage]\r\n> # test step), so the JSON exists. `--tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke` makes the\r\n> # smoke tier SKIP without reading the report, and check.py run-scopes coverage.json\r\n  # (clears a stale copy before the plan), so a prior full-tier report can never be\r\n> # graded as a current pass at the smoke tier (repo-review 2026-07-22 REVIEW-A) —\r\n  # the two together are the belt-and-suspenders fix. Raise a floor (in docs/\r\n  # coverage-floors) only alongside the focused tests that earn it.\r\n> command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_coverage.py --tier {tier} --skip-tiers smoke\r\n  from-stage = DevStg-Impl\r\n  layer = product\r\n  # Serialize AFTER tests+coverage (it reads that step's coverage.json): this\r\n  # repo's CI runs `check.py --jobs 0` (parallel), where an un-laned step would\r\n  # race the producer, find no JSON yet, and SKIP — silently NOT enforcing the\r\n  # floors. `lane` puts it in the tests+coverage lane so it runs once the JSON\r\n  # exists (a no-op at --jobs 1, which is already ordered).\r\n  lane = tests+coverage\r\n> tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py = linecounts\r\n  docs/stage = stage\r\n  docs/open-items.html = openitems\r\n  docs/requirements/components.derived.toml = components\r\n  docs/cli-reference.md = cli | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED CLI REFERENCE --> | <!-- END GENERATED CLI REFERENCE -->\r\n  docs/interface-reference.md = interface-reference | <!-- BEGIN GENERATED INTERFACE REFERENCE --> | <!-- END \r\nGENERATED INTERFACE REFERENCE -->\r\n  docs/ratify/ = approve\r\n  project-trajectory/skills/INDEX.csv = skillsindex\r\n  project-trajectory/prompts/CATALOG.md = promptcatalog\r\n> smoke = python project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py --strict && python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py \r\n--strict && python project-trajectory/scripts/check_docs.py --root . --stale && python scripts/check_resync.py && \r\npython scripts/check_smoke_budget.py --mode warn\r\n> smoke.desc = the per-commit bar: both strict lints, the docs staleness check and the RESYNC entry check, then the \r\nsmoke tier, run once; a red tier fails the bar, and its CPU seconds and wall time are reported against [smoke-budget] \r\nbut judged only by the audit; a lint red stops the bar before the tier runs\r\n> audit = python scripts/check_smoke_budget.py --mode enforce\r\n> audit.desc = the audit's smoke part (review-churn item 12): the smoke tier, run once, judged against [smoke-budget]: \r\nits CPU seconds against cpu-seconds, its wall time against wall-seconds and its test count against max-tests; run by \r\nhand and at phase close, and a breach is filed as a re-tier row, never charged to the lane that ran it\r\n  trajectory = python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict\r\n  trajectory.desc = the trajectory SSOT check, strict\r\n  docs = python project-trajectory/scripts/check_docs.py --root . --stale\r\n  docs.desc = the documentation reference and staleness check\r\n  dashboard = python project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py\r\n  dashboard.desc = regenerate PROJECT_STATE.html (on trunk only: never on a work branch)\r\n  \r\n  [bars]\r\n> # smoke action, so the command has one home. No default: without it `attest`\r\n  # refuses (IF-022).\r\n> review = python project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py smoke\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
