{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -Skip 1670 -First 14; git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -Skip 1969 -First 16; git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:docs/requirements/interfaces.toml | Select-Object -Skip 2401 -First 18; git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^'\"\\\\[need.SN-002\\\\]|\"'^'\"\\\\[need.SN-012\\\\]|\"'^'\"\\\\[need.SN-025\\\\]' -Context 0,12; rg -n 'test_module_size_ratchet' tests/conftest.py\"","aggregated_output":"rationale = \"\"\"The rules read the live source AST instead of parsing the retired docs/architecture.md MODULE MAP block back. One walk, one grammar: the module keys and import names are exactly what the map rendered, so every downstream join (stem resolution, CMP membership) is unchanged.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/gen_arch_map\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/check_trajectory\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"scan_inventory, contracts_grammar_findings, file_contracts, file_grammar_findings, owner_files, ContractsGrammarError\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-006\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"intra-CMP-006 import, declared anyway because gen_arch_map is multi-tagged (CMP-006/CMP-009 via LLR-080) and the multi-tag advisory names an undeclared edge into such a module; the seam of record is honest coverage, not silence. The IF-117 why-an-import-and-not-a-copy reasoning applies verbatim: a second AST walk in the consumer is the silent-divergence hazard a duplicated parser always is.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-132]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The read replaces the retired parse of docs/architecture.md's committed MODULE MAP block - registries/source -> dashboard, no markdown way-station.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/gen_arch_map\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/traj_parse\"]\r\n[interface.IF-199]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"An implementation lands where a commit adds a line declaring it, and a line declares an implementation exactly when the back-link grammar says it does. Calling the grammar the reverse-coverage scan and the module map already read keeps all three agreeing on what a declaration is; a second copy of the rule in the history reader would be a second answer waiting to drift.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/gen_arch_map\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/check_test_first\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"backlink_ids(line) -> the spine ids one line declares · IMPLEMENTS_MARKER · BACKLINK_EXTS\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-006\"\r\nnotes = \"\"\"cross-component crossing declared per the architecture rule: check_test_first is CMP-007 and gen_arch_map is CMP-006/CMP-009.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-200]\r\nrationale = \"\"\"An observation test case's declaration is judged by the same pure join layer as the assumption tier, beside it rather than inside the tier's entry point, because the declaration applies to every case the harness cannot rerun whether or not the frame declares a crossing; drawing the seam here keeps the checker growing by composition lines alone while the rules stay testable on in-memory rows.\"\"\"\r\nowner = \"scripts/assumption_rules\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/trace\", \"scripts/rejudge\", \"scripts/record_observation\", \"scripts/observation_cadence\", \"scripts/gen_release_checklist\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\n[interface.IF-263]\r\nowner = \"scripts/trajectory_arch\"\r\nrequestors = [\"scripts/check_trajectory\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"architecture inventories and findings for seams, contracts, components, code symbols, and spec boundaries\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-006\"\r\n\r\n[interface.IF-264]\r\nowner = \"scripts/schedule\"\r\nconsumers = [\"scripts/traj_status\", \"scripts/agent_brief\", \"scripts/dispatch\", \"scripts/integrate\"]\r\nchannel = \"call\"\r\ndata = \"\"\"work rows and their needs OI edges -> readiness records carrying unmet item ids and titles\"\"\"\r\nversion = \"v1\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nrationale = \"\"\"One readiness calculation projects each unmet open-item edge as a blocked record, so every consumer presents the same held work and item title.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n> [need.SN-002]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\n  need = \"\"\"A reviewer can trust the chain from need to requirement to design to test because it is **mechanically \r\nverified**, not manually asserted: every requirement links to a need and a test before a gate.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"*Requirement-traced … trust what ships*; a hand-maintained trace rots.\"\r\n  priority = \"M\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"The strict traceability check reports **zero orphans** across the joined `SN→SR→LLR→TC` spine; a \r\nmalformed/duplicate id fails at any stage.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-003]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-02\"]\r\n  tags = [\"templates\", \"scripts\"]\r\n  need =\"\"\"A team can use this process on a stack whose tools it declares in a stack profile: it is \r\n**stack-agnostic**, and a non-Python project adopts it by re-pointing the harness at that stack's tools, with Python \r\nonly as the reference.\"\"\"\r\n> [need.SN-012]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-02\"]\r\n  tags = [\"process\"]\r\n  need =\"\"\"A team can keep small changes small: the process is **right-sized**, not ceremony for its own sake — small \r\nchanges stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"An over-heavy process is abandoned; adoption depends on proportionality.\"\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"Opt-in layers (perf, guardrails, unattended, parallel tracks, OKF export) cost a repo that doesn't \r\nuse them nothing; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-023]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\n  # Both tags are INERT AS ROUTING and kept as SUBJECT metadata. `ui`: the UX\r\n> [need.SN-025]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\n  tags = [\"unattended\", \"loop\"]\r\n  need =\"\"\"**With no human curating what comes next**, a configured LLM agent implements toward the vision — fully \r\nautonomously where enabled — deriving its own next work from the repository's tracked state. (The launcher that starts \r\nit is SN-034's obligation; this need is the self-direction.)\"\"\"\r\n  why = \"\"\"Walk-away autonomy is the whole promise of the unattended layer: if a human must hand-maintain a next-step \r\npointer, the loop is attended in practice and the pointer rots against the registry it paraphrases. Extends SN-006 (a \r\n*resumable* unattended run) into a *self-directing* one.\"\"\"\r\n  priority = \"S\"\r\n  acceptance = \"\"\"A plain launch derives what to do next from the tracked WI DAG plus Git — never from prose or a \r\nhand-maintained pointer, and never from predefined tracks; the ready frontier is ordered deterministically, so two \r\nreaders of the same registry dispatch the same work; the status surface a human reads is generated from that tracked \r\nstate, never hand-copied.\"\"\"\r\n  \r\n  [need.SN-026]\r\n  status = \"Approved\"\r\n  stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\r\n  tags = [\"unattended\", \"loop\", \"legal\", \"personal-data\"]\r\n\r\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
