{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^def test_module_sizes_exactly_match_the_committed_baseline'\"' -Context 0,34; git show 30773202c12b2c1abdb16433b6ac4e4b9fe9f826:tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py | Select-String -Pattern '\"'^    \"gen_arch_map.py\"'\"'; rg -n '\"'^SLOW_MODULES|''^def pytest_collection_modifyitems|smoke|slow'\"' tests/conftest.py | Select-Object -Last 18\"","aggregated_output":"\r\n> def test_module_sizes_exactly_match_the_committed_baseline():\r\n      census = _census()\r\n      grew = {\r\n          name: (BASELINE.get(name), lines)\r\n          for name, lines in census.items()\r\n          if lines > BASELINE.get(name, 0)\r\n      }\r\n      improved = {\r\n          name: (baseline, census.get(name))\r\n          for name, baseline in BASELINE.items()\r\n          if census.get(name, 0) < baseline\r\n      }\r\n      message = []\r\n      if grew:\r\n          message.append(\r\n              \"module(s) grew past baseline — decompose (WI-545), do not bump \"\r\n              \"(a deliberate bump is a reviewed baseline edit, reason in the log): \"\r\n              + \"; \".join(\r\n                  \"{} baseline {} -> now {}\".format(\r\n                      name, base or \"absent (new monolith)\", now\r\n                  )\r\n                  for name, (base, now) in sorted(grew.items())\r\n              )\r\n          )\r\n      if improved:\r\n          message.append(\r\n              \"module(s) shrank below baseline — re-stamp these entries downward \"\r\n              \"(or delete them if now <= {}) in this same commit: \".format(THRESHOLD)\r\n              + \"; \".join(\r\n                  \"{} baseline {} -> now {}\".format(\r\n                      name, base, now if now else \"under {}\".format(THRESHOLD + 1)\r\n                  )\r\n                  for name, (base, now) in sorted(improved.items())\r\n              )\r\n          )\r\n    \"gen_arch_map.py\": 1433,  # +39 SLOC (1394 -> 1433) 2026-09-01, WI-543 REVIEW-A round-2 rework: the shared checker \r\nnow diffs bootstrap.delivery_inventory physical sources/reasoned exclusions against MAPPING, inherits generator \r\nreferences for derived outputs, and reports an omitted real shipped source as gate-class missing_file; helper \r\nextraction kept every function below the C901 floor. Reviewed bump, reason in \r\ndocs/log.d/WI-543-sr163-verification-tc.md. Earlier: +53 SLOC (1341 -> 1394) 2026-09-01, WI-543 rework (REVIEW-A \r\nMAJOR): the checker gains a DELIVERED path — `mapping_purpose_over_repo` assembles the real inventory/spine/absences \r\nand grades them, wired into `main` as the warn-first `--mapping-purpose` REPORT MODE (the `--backlink-coverage` \r\nsibling) via `_mapping_purpose_exit` and `_import_sibling`. Before this the checker was reachable only from the test \r\nsuite, so a real unmapped/missing/stale entry produced no report or gate; now TC-204 drives the same delivered \r\nfunction the CLI runs (its `_real_mapping_findings` collapsed onto it) plus two end-to-end subprocess tests. Reviewed \r\nbump, reason in docs/log.d/WI-543-sr163-verification-tc.md. Earlier: +79 SLOC (1262 -> 1341) 2026-08-31, WI-543 \r\n(SR-163, OI-72): the four-class MAPPING-purpose checker lands beside the back-link machinery, LLR-204's module and the \r\npurpose-reference home the ruling names — `mapping_purpose_findings` (the four finding classes: unmapped file, \r\nunresolved reference, missing file, stale entry), `resolve_requirement_reference` (the SR -> live-stakeholder-need \r\njoin stated once), `load_spine_index` (the repo's SR/SN registries), `mapping_purpose_report` (the pass), and \r\n`MAPPING_FINDING_POLICY` (the one warn-vs-gate home). New behavior verified by its own direct TC (TC-204), not \r\nmonolith drift, and the stale arm reuses the same `LIFECYCLE:` marker rule the dogfood walk applies rather than \r\nrestating it. Reviewed bump, reason in docs/log.d/WI-543-sr163-verification-tc.md. Earlier: +7 (2223 -> 2230) \r\n2026-08-29, OI-67 arms pass: `main` composes its three report/splice modes instead of dispatching into the first — six \r\nof the seven lines are the comment stating the defect the shape now prevents (`--cli-doc X --contracts-doc Y --check` \r\nreported X and exited 0 over a stale Y, a green verdict on a document nothing opened). The complexity entry re-stamps \r\nDOWN 21 -> 19 in the same commit. Reviewed bump. Earlier: +30 (2193 -> 2223) 2026-08-29, WI-533 follow-up \r\n(cross-family review F1, CRITICAL): `scan_contracts` gains the PER-SOURCE grammar arm the armed gate needs — pass \r\n`grammar_errors=[]` and a `ContractsGrammarError` from ONE source is recorded as `(source, message)` and the walk \r\ncontinues; pass nothing and it raises exactly as before, so `--contracts-doc` stays loud (a reference that silently \r\nomitted a source it could not parse would report a clean, fresh document over a tree it had not read). The two \r\ncarriers share ONE policy through `_grammar_refusal` rather than writing the arm twice — which also keeps the walk's \r\nbranch count where the complexity ratchet froze it, the decomposition escape that file prefers over a bump. The module \r\nwalk's marker read moved inside the arm too: both halves of the grammar can refuse, and a module that declares NOTHING \r\nis still skipped before its bodies are read, so a bare `Contract IF-###:` in an undeclaring module keeps the refusal \r\nit had. Reviewed bump. Earlier: +10 (2183 -> 2193) 2026-08-29, WI-531 (OI-67 slice 4): DOCSTRING ONLY — the split rows \r\nof OI-67 slice 4 state their `Contract IF-###:` bodies beside the code (IF-150, the --check exit code split off the \r\ncli row IF-010); no executable line changed. Reviewed bump, reason in docs/log.d/2026-08-29-wi531-if-row-split.md. \r\nEarlier: +39 (2144 -> 2183) 2026-08-29, WI-530 (OI-67 slice 3): DOCSTRING ONLY — the `Contract IF-###:` bodies this \r\nmodule owns moved out of the registry cells into its header, the one home the ruling names, and its `Contracts:` \r\nmarker was trimmed to exactly the rows the registry owns to it. No executable line changed. Reviewed bump, reason in \r\ndocs/log.d/2026-08-29-wi530-cell-pass.md. Earlier: +134 (2010 -> 2144) 2026-08-29, WI-529 (OI-67 slice 2): the \r\ncontract header reaches every owner — `header_lines` (the `#` comment block of a TOML/INI/CSV/shell/extensionless \r\nfile, a `#!` line skipped, or the first `<!-- -->` block of a Markdown file), `file_contracts` (the same marker and \r\nbody grammar over those lines; `_contract_bodies` extracted from `module_contract_bodies` so both carriers share ONE \r\nparser), `owner_files` (the registry's file owners, a directory through its README), `file_grammar_findings` \r\n(`_grammar_findings_over` extracted likewise), and `scan_contracts` / `build_contract_reference` / \r\n`_contracts_doc_exit` taking the file owners beside the module walk. NOT decomposed, for the reason this file keeps \r\ngiving: one grammar, one parser, one home — the WI-527 stamp's own argument — and splitting the header reader from the \r\ndocstring reader would put the two carriers of one grammar in two modules. Reviewed bump, reason in \r\ndocs/log.d/2026-08-29-wi529-header-non-python.md. Earlier: RE-STAMPED DOWN -20 (2030 -> 2010) 2026-08-29, WI-528 \r\n(OI-67 ruled (a)): `load_seam_modules` retired with the derivation it served; `_seam_edges` and \r\n`build_dependency_diagram` read the owner off the row and draw the edge the way the information runs (into the owner \r\non a requestors row). Recorded DOWN in the same commit rather than left as headroom, per this file's rule. Earlier: \r\n+348 (1682 -> 2030) 2026-08-29, WI-527 (OI-66 ruled (a)): the contract header's whole mechanism lands in the module \r\nthat already owns the `Contracts:` harvest — the anchored marker grammar (`_MARKER_RE`, `_marker_ids`, \r\n`_marker_text`), the body parser (`module_contract_bodies` and its four refusals), the reference renderer \r\n(`scan_contracts`, `build_contract_reference`) and the `--contracts-doc` mode (`_contracts_doc_exit`). NOT decomposed, \r\nand the reason is the one this file keeps asking for: every piece reads the SAME AST walk over the same docstring, and \r\nsplitting the marker grammar from the body grammar would put two halves of one parser in two modules — the `--cli-doc` \r\nsibling (+189 at WI-512) sits here for the identical reason and is the precedent. Roughly a third of the bump is the \r\nrefusals and their prose: a body before the marker, an undeclared id, a duplicate, and an HTML comment that could \r\nclose the generated document's own end marker. The last +36 is the post-build adversarial round: no UNDECLARED id may \r\nsurvive in the marker's tail (a partial parse declared one seam and dropped the other in silence), a body is ordered \r\nagainst the marker declaring ITS id rather than the first marker in the file, a marker line ends a body so it cannot \r\nbe swallowed into one, and `_md_safe` defangs comment delimiters in BOTH generated references so a module summary \r\ncannot close the document's own end marker. Before that, +2 accepted a TRAILING FULL STOP after the id list, which is \r\nordinary writing and safe where a trailing `and`/comma is not: nothing can follow it, so accepting it cannot drop an \r\nid. Reviewed bump, reason in docs/log.d/2026-08-29-wi527-contract-header.md.\r\n304:        # below with the reason (docs/log.d/2026-08-23-wi496-smoke-retier.md).\r\n360:        # invisible. Profiled with `python -m pytest -q -n 2 -m smoke\r\n378:        # render and refusal cases stay in smoke in test_review_brief.py.\r\n382:        # grammar and the planner's brief stay in smoke in test_plan_pass.py.\r\n417:        # -m smoke --durations=0` (setup+call+teardown per module, the mean of\r\n421:        # exercise keeps a smoke pin elsewhere: kitlib.done_when in\r\n434:        # WI-940: the smoke budget step's real CPU meters.\r\n435:        \"test_smoke_budget_meter\",  # interpreters and CPU burns per case; its in-process pin is test_check_smoke_budget\r\n453:def smoke_tier_for(module_stem):\r\n454:    \"\"\"The tier a test module belongs to: 'slow' for the heavy end-to-end\r\n455:    modules in SLOW_MODULES, else 'smoke'. Total by construction — one test\r\n457:    smoke commit bar leans on).\"\"\"\r\n458:    return \"slow\" if module_stem in SLOW_MODULES else \"smoke\"\r\n461:def pytest_collection_modifyitems(config, items):\r\n464:        item.add_marker(smoke_tier_for(stem))\r\n720:# The declaration lives here rather than in docs/stack.ini (where [smoke-budget]\r\n1703:# One pure function, one traced SN->SR->LLR->TC chain, one marked smoke test.\r\n1728:@pytest.mark.smoke\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
